    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ddd6cda7-88e7-55f9-8b95-4bebd2db545a",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+1,\\,y=4\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+6,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-2\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) & =-2\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}) & =2\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}) & =-2\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}) & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{11\\pi}{6}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
