  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083280,
      "id": "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fdb64555-71e2-5541-9f40-8fb99f7207d2",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1\\cos(3t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})-11,\\,y=3\\sin(3t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+8,\\,\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\le t<\\frac{8\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\sin(3t-\\frac{\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9\\cos(3t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9\\cos(3t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{29\\pi}{18}, \\frac{35\\pi}{18}, \\frac{41\\pi}{18}, \\frac{47\\pi}{18}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{29\\pi}{18}) & =-3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{35\\pi}{18}) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{41\\pi}{18}) & =-3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{47\\pi}{18}) & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{29\\pi}{18}, \\frac{35\\pi}{18}, \\frac{41\\pi}{18}, \\frac{47\\pi}{18}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
