    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8274c183-9161-53b2-979f-ea96143f080a",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos(4t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})-2,\\,y=5\\sin(4t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})-10,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-12\\sin(4t-\\frac{\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=20\\cos(4t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{17\\pi}{24}, \\frac{23\\pi}{24}, \\frac{29\\pi}{24}, \\frac{35\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{17\\pi}{24}) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{23\\pi}{24}) & =12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{29\\pi}{24}) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{35\\pi}{24}) & =12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{17\\pi}{24}, \\frac{23\\pi}{24}, \\frac{29\\pi}{24}, \\frac{35\\pi}{24}.$",
