    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0f22928a-6fea-5d64-9043-088c0e3ee28c",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=1\\cos(4t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})-1,\\,y=5\\sin(4t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})+7,\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\sin(4t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=20\\cos(4t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20\\cos(4t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{19\\pi}{24}, \\frac{25\\pi}{24}, \\frac{31\\pi}{24}, \\frac{37\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{19\\pi}{24}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{25\\pi}{24}) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{31\\pi}{24}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{37\\pi}{24}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{19\\pi}{24}, \\frac{25\\pi}{24}, \\frac{31\\pi}{24}, \\frac{37\\pi}{24}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
