    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "648e34be-2c07-589d-9b69-5baf0b9f31ee",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-8,\\,y=1\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+0,\\,2\\pi\\le t<4\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-12\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}, \\frac{23\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{17\\pi}{6}) & =12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{10\\pi}{3}) & =-12\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{23\\pi}{6}) & =12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}, \\frac{23\\pi}{6}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "48c0d01c-3010-5cb2-bc8b-68fa4403f7f5",
