    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5634b39b-659e-561f-a0a5-318da9c9f47c",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos(3t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})-7,\\,y=5\\sin(3t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})+3,\\,\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\le t<\\frac{8\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-15\\sin(3t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=15\\cos(3t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n15\\cos(3t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{13\\pi}{9}, \\frac{16\\pi}{9}, \\frac{19\\pi}{9}, \\frac{22\\pi}{9}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{13\\pi}{9}) & =15\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{16\\pi}{9}) & =-15\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{19\\pi}{9}) & =15\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{22\\pi}{9}) & =-15\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{13\\pi}{9}, \\frac{16\\pi}{9}, \\frac{19\\pi}{9}, \\frac{22\\pi}{9}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
