      ":question/id": "96618b5b-f90c-5262-b56c-c7e62f9b15eb",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(6t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})-10,\\,y=3\\sin(6t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})-5,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t<\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-24\\sin(6t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=18\\cos(6t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n18\\cos(6t-\\frac{2\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{13\\pi}{36}, \\frac{19\\pi}{36}, \\frac{25\\pi}{36}, \\frac{31\\pi}{36}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{13\\pi}{36}) & =24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{19\\pi}{36}) & =-24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{25\\pi}{36}) & =24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{31\\pi}{36}) & =-24\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{13\\pi}{36}, \\frac{19\\pi}{36}, \\frac{25\\pi}{36}, \\frac{31\\pi}{36}.$",
