  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083280,
      "id": "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4d20c378-153f-5b81-9d8c-b5ee08e053fd",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-4,\\,y=4\\sin(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-2,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-9\\sin(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12\\cos(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n12\\cos(3t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\frac{5\\pi}{12}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi}{12}) & =9\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi}{12}) & =-9\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}) & =9\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{13\\pi}{12}) & =-9\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\frac{5\\pi}{12}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "502051c6-098b-569e-bb3f-fea3fe2f9172",
