      ":question/id": "99e010e1-1f5f-5cfb-8c85-6d076e71be81",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+8,\\,y=1\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})+11,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-30\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{23\\pi}{36}, \\frac{29\\pi}{36}, \\frac{35\\pi}{36}, \\frac{41\\pi}{36}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{23\\pi}{36}) & =30\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{29\\pi}{36}) & =-30\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{35\\pi}{36}) & =30\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{41\\pi}{36}) & =-30\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{23\\pi}{36}, \\frac{29\\pi}{36}, \\frac{35\\pi}{36}, \\frac{41\\pi}{36}.$",
