    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e6b3bcc8-704d-5483-8629-7ddacf5af527",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-11,\\,y=3\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})-1,\\,\\frac{5\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{9\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-8\\sin(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\cos(2t-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{21\\pi}{8}, \\frac{25\\pi}{8}, \\frac{29\\pi}{8}, \\frac{33\\pi}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{21\\pi}{8}) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{25\\pi}{8}) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{29\\pi}{8}) & =-8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{33\\pi}{8}) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{21\\pi}{8}, \\frac{25\\pi}{8}, \\frac{29\\pi}{8}, \\frac{33\\pi}{8}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
