    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "006c2d4d-5d1d-5520-b3a7-bd2c4862388a",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+6,\\,y=6\\sin(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-6,\\,\\pi\\le t<\\frac{9\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-20\\sin(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=30\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n30\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{17\\pi}{15}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{23\\pi}{15}, \\frac{26\\pi}{15}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{17\\pi}{15}) & =20\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}) & =-20\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{23\\pi}{15}) & =20\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{26\\pi}{15}) & =-20\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{17\\pi}{15}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{23\\pi}{15}, \\frac{26\\pi}{15}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
