    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ab801bf9-12ae-5420-8146-728ea2ec6df5",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})+9,\\,y=6\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})-5,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le t<\\frac{5\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-36\\sin(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=36\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n36\\cos(6t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{24}, \\frac{11\\pi}{24}, \\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{19\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi}{24}) & =36\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi}{24}) & =-36\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi}{8}) & =36\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{19\\pi}{24}) & =-36\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{7\\pi}{24}, \\frac{11\\pi}{24}, \\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{19\\pi}{24}.$",
